      "text": "Let's denote the initial pressure as \\( P_1 = 5 \\) kPa, the initial volume as \\( V_1 = 2.28 \\) liters, and the new volume as \\( V_2 = 5.7 \\) liters. We are asked to find the new pressure \\( P_2 \\) when the gas is moved to a different volume while maintaining constant temperature.\n\nThe relationship between pressure and volume at constant temperature is given by Boyle's Law, which states that the product of pressure and volume is constant. Mathematically, this can be expressed as:\n\\[ P_1 V_1 = P_2 V_2 \\]\n\nWe can rearrange this equation to solve for \\( P_2 \\):\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{P_1 V_1}{V_2} \\]\n\nSubstituting the given values into the equation:\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{5 \\text{ kPa} \\times 2.28 \\text{ liters}}{5.7 \\text{ liters}} \\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication and division:\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{11.4 \\text{ kPa} \\cdot \\text{liters}}{5.7 \\text{ liters}} \\]\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{11.4}{5.7} \\text{ kPa} \\]\n\\[ P_2 \\approx 2 \\text{ kPa} \\]\n\nTherefore, the new pressure when the gas is moved to a 5.7 liter container is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2} \\] kPa.",
